WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.780
You know, you usually expect the food on your plate to have like a biological story, right?

00:04.940 --> 00:06.160
Right, a specific origin.

00:06.480 --> 00:11.860
Yeah, exactly. Like a clear traceable lineage back to a living organism out in the dirt or in a pasture somewhere.

00:12.360 --> 00:16.600
An apple comes from a tree. A steak is a muscle from a cow. A potato is a tuber.

00:16.980 --> 00:22.980
And even when food is heavily processed, we are still fundamentally dealing with botanical or zoological anatomies.

00:22.980 --> 00:29.800
Right. Anatomies that nature designed. And we're just kind of working around those biological constraints.

00:30.620 --> 00:35.620
It's comforting, honestly, because we really like things to be categorical, plant or animal.

00:36.040 --> 00:38.120
We do. We like neat boxes for what we eat.

00:38.620 --> 00:47.340
But when you step into the absolute bleeding edge of advanced food engineering, that entire biological origin story is, well, it's being completely upended.

00:47.580 --> 00:54.920
We're looking at a manufacturing landscape that doesn't care if an ingredient came from a cow, a soybean, or like a wild cattail.

00:54.920 --> 01:04.540
It is entirely agnostic to the source. And that's really the central premise we are exploring today from this, I mean, it's an incredibly dense, highly technical 2026 monograph.

01:04.760 --> 01:05.920
Oh, it is a heavy read.

01:06.000 --> 01:13.680
Yeah, it's by the independent researcher known as Phlyxion. The paper is titled Phase Fraction and Filament Geometry in Structured Foods.

01:13.800 --> 01:16.020
Which sounds incredibly dry. I know.

01:16.020 --> 01:20.200
It does. It reads like a mechanical engineering thesis crossed with a materials science textbook.

01:20.520 --> 01:26.540
But what it actually represents is a blueprint for a completely new configurable food manufacturing platform.

01:26.800 --> 01:33.380
Right. It aims to completely decouple the texture and flavor of what you eat from the original agricultural source.

01:33.380 --> 01:36.980
Exactly. The goal here isn't to write a new recipe for a veggie burger.

01:37.260 --> 01:48.280
No, not at all. The monograph is proposing that we can use principles from, this is wild, optical fiber manufacturing, traditional candy making, and composite materials.

01:48.400 --> 01:50.260
Like the carbon fiber used in aerospace, yeah.

01:50.420 --> 02:00.220
Right. To engineer the precise shredding texture of meat, or the burst of juiciness in a piece of fruit, using entirely interchangeable streams of plant matter.

02:00.220 --> 02:08.480
So if you are at all curious about what is literally going to be sitting on your dinner plate in the year 2030, this deep dive is for you.

02:08.740 --> 02:13.380
The architecture of how we eat is being redesigned from the microscopic level up.

02:13.980 --> 02:21.640
And the author starts this redesign by completely dismantling our traditional culinary taxonomy, which I found fascinating.

02:22.020 --> 02:24.840
We usually categorize a dish by its main ingredient.

02:25.480 --> 02:28.060
Right. You'd say chicken and rice or beef stew.

02:28.060 --> 02:36.980
Yeah. But this monograph proposes that a massive portion of the world's prepared savory dishes can actually be reduced to just three structural elements.

02:37.280 --> 02:38.560
The three basic building blocks.

02:38.840 --> 02:42.840
Right. It calls them the carrier, the matrix, and the inclusions.

02:43.440 --> 02:47.680
I found this framework so useful for just, you know, looking at a menu differently.

02:47.960 --> 02:51.320
The carrier is essentially your starch-based body.

02:51.480 --> 02:52.100
It's the bulk.

02:52.100 --> 02:55.580
Exactly. It supplies the physical satiation and the bite.

02:56.040 --> 02:59.420
This is your bread, your rice, noodles, flatbreads, dumpling wrappers.

02:59.560 --> 03:00.800
It's the physical foundation.

03:01.420 --> 03:03.020
And then you have the matrix, right?

03:03.120 --> 03:03.720
Yes, the matrix.

03:04.160 --> 03:08.660
That's the liquid or semi-liquid phase, the sauce, the gravy, the binding fat.

03:08.820 --> 03:12.920
It carries the salt, the acid, and most importantly, the aroma.

03:13.500 --> 03:14.880
The flavor delivery system.

03:15.080 --> 03:15.320
Right.

03:15.320 --> 03:16.840
And finally, inclusions.

03:17.100 --> 03:21.360
These are just the discrete, identifiable pieces distributed through that matrix.

03:21.520 --> 03:24.800
So a chunk of vegetable, a legume, a piece of cheese, or meat.

03:25.500 --> 03:32.580
So when you abstract food this way, a pizza is really just a baked carrier with a tomato matrix and cheese inclusions.

03:32.960 --> 03:33.480
Exactly.

03:34.200 --> 03:38.660
A curry is a highly spiced matrix served over a rice carrier.

03:38.660 --> 03:45.400
A sandwich, a burrito, a dumpling, they're all just different topological arrangements of a carrier in a matrix.

03:45.780 --> 03:49.920
They are stacked or wrapped or enclosed, but structurally, it's the same concept.

03:50.520 --> 03:55.060
Okay, let's unpack this, because I like to think of a dish like a video game engine.

03:55.500 --> 03:56.900
Oh, that's a good way to look at it.

03:57.000 --> 03:57.260
Right.

03:57.620 --> 04:03.020
So the carrier, the protein or starch base, is the physics engine running in the background.

04:03.020 --> 04:09.460
It handles the collision detection, the gravity, the basic mechanical resistance when your teeth hit it.

04:09.760 --> 04:10.560
The structural math.

04:10.780 --> 04:10.940
Yeah.

04:11.240 --> 04:16.180
And the matrix, the sauce, the fat, the spices, that's the graphics and the rendering.

04:16.560 --> 04:19.360
It's what your senses are immediately reacting to.

04:19.860 --> 04:23.400
And the author introduces this idea called the late-binding hypothesis.

04:23.740 --> 04:25.440
Which is a huge paradigm shift.

04:25.600 --> 04:26.220
It really is.

04:26.220 --> 04:33.000
It basically argues that if the graphics and rendering are complex enough, you don't even notice what physics engine is running underneath.

04:33.180 --> 04:33.540
Exactly.

04:33.820 --> 04:44.020
Hypothesis 1 states that beyond a certain threshold of flavor complexity, the perceived identity of a savory dish is determined almost entirely by its final aroma, its sauce, and its fat profile.

04:44.460 --> 04:48.020
Meaning, the taster cannot reliably identify the underlying protein base.

04:48.180 --> 04:48.440
Right.

04:48.860 --> 04:51.140
And there is deep sensory science backing this up.

04:51.140 --> 04:56.700
The monograph dives into human aroma perception, specifically a phenomenon known as configural perception.

04:57.420 --> 04:57.500
Right.

04:57.600 --> 05:00.440
Because we don't taste 30 different things at once.

05:00.840 --> 05:02.360
There's a biological bandwidth limit.

05:02.740 --> 05:03.260
Precisely.

05:03.560 --> 05:08.620
When a perfumer or a chef mixes two or three distinct odors, you can often pick them out.

05:08.840 --> 05:10.940
You smell vanilla and you smell cinnamon.

05:11.140 --> 05:11.280
Sure.

05:11.460 --> 05:11.940
They're distinct.

05:11.940 --> 05:24.300
But when you mix 10 to 50 volatile compounds together, like you would in a complex curry, a mole, or a really rich demi-glace, your brain simply cannot parse the individual notes anymore.

05:24.540 --> 05:25.360
It's too much data.

05:25.600 --> 05:25.840
Yeah.

05:26.060 --> 05:30.040
You don't taste cumin plus coriander plus turmeric plus allium.

05:30.480 --> 05:37.320
Your olfactory bulb reaches a processing limit and your brain synthesizes a single, entirely new complex odor.

05:37.680 --> 05:39.500
It becomes a new object in your mind.

05:39.500 --> 05:43.540
It crosses a complexity threshold and becomes its own distinct entity.

05:44.120 --> 05:49.860
And the text actually points out that domestic cooks have intuitively understood configural perception for centuries.

05:50.180 --> 05:50.660
Oh, yeah.

05:51.040 --> 06:00.960
There's a protocol outlined in the text that blew my mind, where if you take a completely bland-based textured vegetable protein or boiled lentils or even day-old bread.

06:01.200 --> 06:02.980
Literally just old bread.

06:02.980 --> 06:03.660
Right.

06:03.660 --> 06:10.080
And you hydrate it in a rich broth and fry it in fat with onions, herbs, and a complex spice mixture.

06:10.560 --> 06:13.080
You overwrite its perceived identity entirely.

06:13.420 --> 06:14.640
You aren't eating bread anymore.

06:14.800 --> 06:16.160
You are just eating the dish.

06:16.480 --> 06:18.080
The matrix dominates the carrier.

06:18.480 --> 06:22.520
Which leads directly into how the monograph handles the concept of meatiness.

06:22.520 --> 06:26.340
Yes, this is a major departure from current industry trends.

06:26.860 --> 06:33.700
For the last decade, the alternative protein industry has treated meat flavor as this elusive, magical property of animal muscle.

06:34.200 --> 06:39.760
Something that has to be painstakingly replicated, often by genetically engineering specific proteins.

06:40.260 --> 06:41.840
But this text strips all that away.

06:42.060 --> 06:44.860
It defines meatiness simply as a reaction class.

06:45.240 --> 06:48.700
It's primarily driven by Browning chemistry, the Maillard reaction.

06:48.700 --> 06:52.360
Which is just heat acting on sugars and amino acids, right?

06:52.620 --> 06:52.960
Exactly.

06:53.560 --> 06:56.700
And specifically, sulfur-bearing amino acids like cysteine.

06:57.780 --> 07:00.500
Which, importantly, isn't exclusive to animals at all.

07:00.740 --> 07:01.520
Not even slightly.

07:02.320 --> 07:02.760
Alliums.

07:02.940 --> 07:06.400
So onions, garlic, leeks, they are packed with sulfur compounds.

07:06.880 --> 07:18.480
So when you fry onions in a pan with oil and a bit of sugar, you're triggering a massive cascade of the exact same sulfur-derived heterocyclic browning notes that you get when you sear a steak on a grill.

07:18.700 --> 07:19.920
It's the same chemistry.

07:20.400 --> 07:28.620
The plant-based industry has made such a massive deal out of heme, the iron-containing molecule in blood, acting as the magic bullet that makes fake meat taste real.

07:28.740 --> 07:28.960
Right.

07:29.040 --> 07:30.520
The impossible burger approach.

07:30.800 --> 07:31.100
Yes.

07:31.360 --> 07:36.820
But this researcher essentially downgrades heme from being the source of meatiness to just being a catalyst.

07:36.820 --> 07:39.240
That's a crucial distinction in the text.

07:40.040 --> 07:41.420
Iron is a transition metal.

07:41.860 --> 07:49.780
In these flavor reactions, its primary role is to lower the activation energy required for those sulfur and sugar reactions to occur during cooking.

07:50.200 --> 07:53.160
It speeds the chemistry up and pushes it down certain pathways.

07:53.380 --> 07:53.640
Right.

07:54.200 --> 08:00.580
It is one highly effective route into the meatiness reaction class, but it is not the source of the flavor itself.

08:00.580 --> 08:04.020
The implication for food manufacturing here is huge.

08:04.260 --> 08:04.880
Oh, massive.

08:05.500 --> 08:15.760
Because if meatiness is just a late-stage chemical reaction class driven by configural perception, it means you do not need to build the flavor into the structure of a fake meat.

08:16.180 --> 08:20.500
You don't need to genetically engineer your pea plants to express flavor compounds in their roots.

08:20.680 --> 08:21.060
Exactly.

08:22.040 --> 08:24.300
Flavor belongs entirely at the end of the assembly line.

08:24.300 --> 08:29.940
You build the physical structure first, and you apply the flavor at the finishing stage.

08:30.060 --> 08:31.920
That is the essence of late-binding.

08:32.440 --> 08:37.400
But wait, I have to push back here because it feels like there is a massive blind spot in this theory.

08:37.600 --> 08:38.580
Okay, let's hear it.

08:39.060 --> 08:40.860
Aroma does a lot of heavy lifting, sure.

08:42.000 --> 08:45.580
But the human jaw is an incredibly sensitive mechanical instrument.

08:45.580 --> 08:54.500
We have millions of years of evolutionary training designed to detect rotten, spongy, or incorrect textures in our food as a survival mechanism.

08:55.140 --> 08:56.400
That's very true.

08:56.840 --> 09:05.680
So if you take soaked bread and cover it in a mathematically perfect configural curry sauce, I am still going to know I am biting into mush, not chicken.

09:06.320 --> 09:07.680
The illusion breaks instantly.

09:08.080 --> 09:13.020
You are touching on the exact limitation that the author explicitly states.

09:13.020 --> 09:18.780
The late-binding hypothesis has a hard failure condition, and that is texture conflict.

09:19.620 --> 09:21.600
So aroma can't hide a mechanical failure.

09:21.960 --> 09:22.760
No, it cannot.

09:23.300 --> 09:31.860
If the base has the wrong fracture behavior, if it yields to your molars like a sponge instead of shredding like a muscle, the taster's brain flags it immediately.

09:32.380 --> 09:35.560
The illusion doesn't just degrade. It shatters completely.

09:36.180 --> 09:40.740
So if the physics engine glitches, it doesn't matter how pretty the graphics are.

09:40.740 --> 09:42.720
You still fall through the floor of the map.

09:43.020 --> 09:44.300
A perfect way to put it.

09:44.300 --> 09:51.600
And that failure condition is precisely why the author dedicates the vast majority of this monograph to solving the texture problem.

09:51.980 --> 09:53.820
Because you have to get the physics engine right.

09:54.040 --> 09:54.440
Exactly.

09:54.840 --> 09:58.980
And the solution isn't to just keep tweaking the hydration of a pulverized protein paste.

09:59.540 --> 10:04.020
To mimic muscle, you have to actually look at the structural reference of muscle itself.

10:04.020 --> 10:06.700
Which is a marvel of biological engineering.

10:07.140 --> 10:07.960
It really is.

10:08.320 --> 10:16.320
Let's drill into the anatomy of a muscle, because understanding this makes you realize why every plant-based chicken nugget you've ever had feels slightly wrong.

10:16.720 --> 10:18.520
A muscle isn't just a block of protein.

10:18.520 --> 10:24.580
It's like a fractal geometry of tubes wrapped inside other tubes.

10:25.080 --> 10:26.640
It's a highly ordered hierarchy.

10:27.280 --> 10:33.660
At the smallest level, you have a single muscle fiber, which is wrapped in a very thin sheath of connective tissue called the endomysium.

10:33.660 --> 10:36.520
Okay, so a fiber in a sheath.

10:36.880 --> 10:37.040
Right.

10:37.600 --> 10:41.360
Then those individual fibers are bundled together into groups called fascicles.

10:41.800 --> 10:46.160
Those fascicles are wrapped in a thicker sheath of connective tissue called the paramecium.

10:46.500 --> 10:49.700
So bundles of tubes inside bigger tubes.

10:50.100 --> 10:50.440
Yes.

10:50.700 --> 10:56.600
And then all of those bundles are grouped together to form the whole muscle, which is wrapped in an outer layer called the epamecium.

10:56.600 --> 11:03.340
And this specific geometry is what dictates the entire mechanical experience of eating meat, right?

11:03.760 --> 11:06.440
The direction those fibers run gives a cut of meat.

11:06.620 --> 11:07.480
It's grain.

11:08.280 --> 11:08.600
Exactly.

11:09.120 --> 11:14.040
When you bite into a steak, you're either biting parallel to those tubes or perpendicular across them.

11:14.300 --> 11:16.200
And the resistance is completely different.

11:16.760 --> 11:22.780
Plus that connective tissue, the paramecium around the bundles, that creates intentional weak planes.

11:22.780 --> 11:32.680
When you slow cook a pork shoulder and it falls apart into those beautiful, distinct shreds, it is literally tearing along those paramecium planes because the collagen is melted.

11:33.520 --> 11:42.420
Furthermore, when you look at intramuscular fat, the marbling that gives high-quality meat its juiciness, that fat isn't just randomly emulsified into the protein.

11:42.720 --> 11:43.640
No, it's structural.

11:43.920 --> 11:44.140
Right.

11:44.480 --> 11:50.320
It is deposited in discrete pockets, largely in those spaces between the fascicle bundles along the connective tissue.

11:50.320 --> 11:58.140
So if you are a food engineer trying to replicate this, you can't just extrude a homogenous block of soy protein and hope it feels right.

11:58.740 --> 12:00.440
You need discrete compartments.

12:00.680 --> 12:02.080
You need a directional grain.

12:02.360 --> 12:05.000
You need built-in weak planes so it shreds correctly.

12:05.420 --> 12:08.820
And you need isolated pockets of fat that burst when you chew.

12:09.060 --> 12:14.300
And to achieve this, the monograph proposes what it calls the core shell strand process.

12:14.440 --> 12:17.480
Which is really the mechanical heart of a whole monograph.

12:17.900 --> 12:18.320
It is.

12:18.320 --> 12:26.820
The traditional approach in food tech for creating fibers usually involves microscopic 3D printing nozzles or shear cell extrusion.

12:27.120 --> 12:30.740
Trying to force paste through tiny holes to make individual threads.

12:30.980 --> 12:31.280
Right.

12:31.480 --> 12:33.140
It's incredibly slow.

12:33.460 --> 12:34.680
It clogs constantly.

12:34.960 --> 12:37.120
And it's a nightmare to scale up industrially.

12:37.120 --> 12:45.400
So the author completely bypasses the microscopic nozzle problem by looking at how we manufacture entirely different materials.

12:45.400 --> 12:48.640
They propose building what is called a preform.

12:49.120 --> 12:52.760
To visualize a preform, imagine you are building a very thick rope.

12:52.940 --> 12:54.980
Maybe the diameter of a garden hose.

12:55.200 --> 12:55.420
Okay.

12:55.620 --> 12:56.120
Pretty big.

12:56.400 --> 12:56.640
Yeah.

12:56.900 --> 12:59.560
You have a soft core material running down the center.

12:59.740 --> 13:03.580
This could be a liquid fat, a flavored juice, or a soft emulsion.

13:04.080 --> 13:09.580
You wrap that core in a firm, formable outer shell made of a plant protein dough.

13:09.580 --> 13:13.000
So it's basically a giant tube of dough filled with fat.

13:13.460 --> 13:13.820
Exactly.

13:14.260 --> 13:16.840
At this macro scale, it's very easy to handle.

13:17.280 --> 13:22.740
You can perfectly control the ratio of the core to the shell without any high-precision microscopic equipment.

13:23.080 --> 13:25.900
The analogies the text draws here are fantastic.

13:26.400 --> 13:30.820
This is exactly the mechanical process used by traditional confectioners to make hard candies.

13:30.820 --> 13:35.980
Like those old-fashioned candies that have a soft fruit center surrounded by a hard sugar shell?

13:36.180 --> 13:36.580
Exactly.

13:37.160 --> 13:39.540
You don't inject the center into a tiny hard shell.

13:39.940 --> 13:44.900
You build a massive cylinder of soft filling inside a massive cylinder of sugar dough, and then you pull it.

13:45.260 --> 13:48.920
You physically stretch it out until it's thin, and then you chop it into pieces.

13:49.520 --> 13:54.240
It's the exact same principle used in manufacturing optical glass fibers for telecommunications.

13:54.800 --> 13:57.980
They don't try to cast a glass wire the thickness of a human hair.

13:57.980 --> 14:03.540
No, they build a large glass cylinder, highly controlled, with a specific core-to-cladding ratio.

14:03.800 --> 14:10.800
Then they put it in a draw tower, heat the glass to its softening point, and physically pull it down into a fiber that is kilometers long.

14:11.160 --> 14:15.240
And this brings us to one of the core mathematical principles in the text.

14:15.900 --> 14:18.340
Hypothesis 3, draw invariance.

14:19.080 --> 14:21.840
This is the physics that makes the whole platform viable.

14:21.840 --> 14:40.260
Right. Draw invariance states that if you take this thick preform, this garden hose-sized tube of protein wrapped around a core of fat, and you stretch it out to a fraction of a millimeter in diameter, the ratio of the core's area to the total cross-sectional area remains mathematically identical.

14:40.260 --> 14:44.460
It's a pure expression of volume conservation.

14:44.980 --> 14:56.340
If your core made up 20% of the cross-section when the preform was two inches thick, it will still make up exactly 20% of the cross-section when the strand is drawn down to the thickness of a sewing thread.

14:57.120 --> 15:09.100
What's fascinating here is that you get to set your highly precise proportions at a macroscopic scale, where you can easily see and control them, and you just let the physics of drawing do the microscopic scaling for you.

15:09.100 --> 15:14.620
But let's pause and look at the fluid dynamics of this, because I was deeply skeptical when reading this part.

15:14.880 --> 15:16.300
Oh, the capillary breakup?

15:16.700 --> 15:16.980
Yeah.

15:17.460 --> 15:32.420
Because if my core is a liquid emulsion, say, a highly concentrated oat milk and sunflower oil mixture, and my shell is a protein dough, and I stretch this entire thing until it's the thickness of a hair, liquids don't like to be stretched like that.

15:32.680 --> 15:33.740
They really don't.

15:33.740 --> 15:35.640
Think about water falling from a faucet.

15:36.020 --> 15:43.900
It starts as a continuous cylinder, but as it stretches and accelerates, surface tension causes it to pinch off and break into individual drops.

15:44.760 --> 15:47.540
Doesn't the liquid core just snap inside the shell?

15:47.880 --> 15:53.600
You're describing a phenomenon in fluid dynamics known as the Rayleigh Plateau instability, or capillary breakup.

15:53.960 --> 15:55.180
Okay, capillary breakup.

15:55.180 --> 16:04.340
Yeah. A cylinder of liquid is inherently unstable, because it naturally wants to minimize its surface area, and a sphere has less surface area than a cylinder.

16:04.740 --> 16:07.180
So it breaks apart to form little spheres.

16:07.480 --> 16:12.780
Right. So under tension, small perturbations grow until the liquid pinches off into droplets.

16:13.340 --> 16:17.220
And you're right. The author explicitly anticipates this happening during the drawdown.

16:17.220 --> 16:26.860
Wait. So the continuous tube of fat just disintegrates into a dotted line inside the strand. I would think that ruins the structural integrity of the food.

16:27.080 --> 16:35.200
Well, that's where the engineering perspective shifts. The text argues this is not a defect. It is a highly desirable feature.

16:35.560 --> 16:36.500
Really? How?

16:36.500 --> 16:41.960
If capillary breakup occurs, you no longer have a continuous tube of fat running the length of the meat.

16:42.360 --> 16:49.420
You have a beaded core, a chain of discrete microscopic liquid droplets trapped inside the protein shell.

16:49.420 --> 16:52.500
Oh, wow. You have just simulated marbling.

16:52.940 --> 17:01.780
Exactly. You've created tiny, isolated pockets of fat or juice that will only burst when mechanical pressure, like a human tooth, ruptures the shell.

17:02.000 --> 17:03.620
That is genuinely brilliant.

17:03.980 --> 17:05.960
It is a very elegant solution.

17:05.960 --> 17:13.060
You are weaponizing fluid instability to recreate the exact burst-in-the-mouth sensation of animal fat.

17:13.260 --> 17:14.900
It's a very elegant solution.

17:15.340 --> 17:20.940
But for any of this to work, that outer protein shell has to be engineered flawlessly. It can't just be regular dough.

17:21.200 --> 17:29.620
No, it can't. The author refers to this as the shell-setting problem, and it is arguably the most difficult material science challenge in the monograph.

17:29.820 --> 17:35.340
Because the shell has to satisfy three completely contradictory physical requirements simultaneously.

17:35.340 --> 17:38.400
Right. Let's go through them. First, containment.

17:38.400 --> 17:47.680
Right. Containment. As you are drawing it down, and the core is undergoing capillary breakup, the shell has to hold that liquid in without rupturing or leaking.

17:48.680 --> 17:59.400
Second, flexibility. Once you draw this strand, you have to wind it onto a spool to store it or process it. If the shell is too rigid, it snaps like dry pasta.

17:59.400 --> 18:11.900
And third, bondability. Later in the process, you are going to need these strands to stick to each other to form a solid block of food. If the outside of the shell is completely smooth and cured, it won't bond.

18:12.340 --> 18:17.620
So how do they navigate these contradictions? Because they seem impossible to reconcile.

18:17.620 --> 18:22.940
The text outlines several distinct setting methods. The simplest is thermal setting.

18:23.480 --> 18:25.160
So just applying heat.

18:25.520 --> 18:31.900
Right. You pass the drawn strand through a brief, intense heat source, like a steam tunnel or an infrared array.

18:32.080 --> 18:32.360
Okay.

18:32.360 --> 18:43.840
This denatures the proteins on the very outermost layer of the shell, forming a temporary skin that contains the core, while leaving the inner wall of the shell undenatured so it can still bond later.

18:44.400 --> 18:50.220
Oh, I see. It's like searing a microscopic steak just enough to give it a crust, but keeping it raw inside.

18:50.600 --> 18:58.040
Exactly. But they also detail a method called ionic setting, which I found fascinating because it borrows heavily from molecular gastronomy.

18:58.040 --> 19:01.940
Yes, the sodium alginate part. This was so cool.

19:02.580 --> 19:07.180
Yeah. This leverages the chemistry of sodium alginate, a compound derived from brown algae.

19:07.500 --> 19:11.660
You incorporate sodium alginate into the protein dough of your shell formulation.

19:12.260 --> 19:18.920
And then as the strand is drawn out, you pass it through a liquid bath containing calcium ions, usually calcium chloride.

19:19.480 --> 19:22.640
Which is exactly how chefs make fake caviar out of fruit juice.

19:22.820 --> 19:23.280
Yes.

19:23.280 --> 19:33.220
The moment the alginate hits the calcium, the calcium ions act like little chemical bridges, cross-linking the alginate polymer chains together to instantly form a gel.

19:33.760 --> 19:37.820
It creates a flexible, highly contained skin almost instantaneously.

19:38.360 --> 19:42.520
It is incredibly effective for the first two requirements, containment and flexibility.

19:43.100 --> 19:46.520
You can wind that strand around a spool all day and it won't leak or snap.

19:46.520 --> 19:54.840
But it fails the third requirement completely, right? Because the outside is a fully cross-linked alginate gel. It has no reactive sites left.

19:55.520 --> 20:00.840
Right. It won't stick to another strand later on. It's like trying to glue two pieces of wet silicone together.

20:01.260 --> 20:11.060
Which is why the text suggests hybrid approaches. They explore frying, passing the strand rapidly through hot oil, which imparts a tremendous amount of complex Maillard flavor.

20:11.060 --> 20:12.760
And sets a firm skin.

20:13.520 --> 20:17.280
Though it makes the strand quite brittle when it cools down, so that's a trade-off.

20:17.500 --> 20:17.760
True.

20:18.280 --> 20:25.640
They also explore drying, using high-velocity air to essentially desiccate the outer few microns of the shell.

20:26.320 --> 20:37.080
You can handle the dry strand, spool it, and then later you just mist it with a solvent or water to rehydrate that outer layer and recover its stickiness right before you need it to bond.

20:37.080 --> 20:44.140
The sheer amount of iterative testing required to balance containment, flexibility, and bondability is staggering.

20:45.040 --> 20:55.360
But let's assume we have solved it. We have our draw tower working, we have drawn down these preforms, and we now have kilometers of these tiny core shell filaments.

20:56.040 --> 20:58.880
Fat cores inside flexible protein shells.

20:59.420 --> 21:01.340
And they are on a spool.

21:01.720 --> 21:06.840
Right. What is the next mechanical step to turn that thread into a stake?

21:07.080 --> 21:11.840
We move from the creation of the strand to the assembly of the macro structure.

21:12.480 --> 21:15.340
The text refers to this phase as coiling and consolidation.

21:15.940 --> 21:17.960
Coiling and consolidation. Okay.

21:18.220 --> 21:21.480
The core mechanism here is a rotating cylinder called a mandrel.

21:22.040 --> 21:25.280
So imagine a thick metal rolling pin constantly spinning.

21:25.900 --> 21:30.140
The spool of strand is fed onto the spinning mandrel by a traverse guide,

21:30.140 --> 21:35.940
a mechanical arm that moves back and forth along the length of the cylinder, laying the strand down layer by layer.

21:35.940 --> 21:37.960
And here's where it gets really interesting.

21:38.520 --> 21:44.700
By precisely controlling the speed at which that traverse guide moves back and forth relative to the rotational speed of the mandrel,

21:44.900 --> 21:47.940
you dictate what the text calls the helix angle.

21:48.200 --> 21:49.140
The helix angle.

21:49.520 --> 21:53.980
And this angle determines the entire mechanical behavior of the final food product.

21:54.440 --> 21:55.960
Let's visualize this for a second.

21:56.180 --> 21:56.460
Go ahead.

21:56.460 --> 22:01.740
If the mandrel is spinning very fast, but the traverse guide is moving incredibly slowly,

22:02.400 --> 22:05.760
the strand is just being laid down directly next to itself over and over.

22:06.300 --> 22:07.480
Every strand is parallel.

22:08.000 --> 22:09.660
The text calls this hoop coiling.

22:10.160 --> 22:11.740
It looks exactly like a spool of thread.

22:12.220 --> 22:17.120
However, if you speed up the traverse guide so it zips back and forth quickly while the mandrel spins,

22:17.360 --> 22:19.160
the strands don't lay parallel.

22:19.160 --> 22:24.220
They cross over each other at an angle, laying down in a helical pattern, layer after layer.

22:24.780 --> 22:26.840
This isn't a technique invented for food, by the way.

22:26.980 --> 22:31.540
This is exactly how aerospace engineers manufacture filament-wound composite structures,

22:31.820 --> 22:35.180
like carbon fiber pressure vessels or rocket motor casings.

22:35.640 --> 22:41.700
And the reason this matters so much for food is that the angle completely dictates how the structure yields to your teeth.

22:41.700 --> 22:49.600
Exactly. If you use a high helix angle, creating highly crossed, interwoven layers, the structure deeply resists separating.

22:49.940 --> 22:51.200
The fibers act like a net.

22:51.600 --> 22:52.280
It bites back.

22:52.480 --> 22:52.700
Right.

22:53.200 --> 22:55.540
It requires high compressive force to chew through it.

22:55.840 --> 22:58.840
It feels dense and chewy, more like a tough cut of beef.

22:59.400 --> 23:03.980
Conversely, if you use parallel hoop coiling, the strands have no mechanical interlock.

23:04.240 --> 23:06.620
They only rely on the chemical bond of the shells.

23:06.620 --> 23:11.400
So when you apply shear force, like pulling it with a fork or tearing it with your incisors,

23:11.800 --> 23:14.780
the strands want to pull apart from each other along their length.

23:15.340 --> 23:17.160
This is how you engineer shred.

23:17.720 --> 23:22.120
If you want to replicate pulled pork or the distinct fibrous tear of cooked poultry,

23:22.480 --> 23:26.380
you use hoop coiling to program those parallel failure planes into the architecture.

23:26.780 --> 23:30.820
But you can't just wind a bunch of cylindrical strands onto a tube and call it a day,

23:31.140 --> 23:33.320
because of the geometry of circles.

23:33.320 --> 23:39.700
Right. Even if you pack round strands together as perfectly and densely as mathematically possible,

23:40.280 --> 23:44.960
like a hexagonal close packing arrangement, circles do not tessellate perfectly.

23:45.380 --> 23:49.440
Right. The math tells us that in a perfect hexagonal packing of identical cylinders,

23:49.660 --> 23:52.020
you still have about 9% void space.

23:52.580 --> 23:57.560
And in real world, slightly irregular coiling, that void space can be up to 22%.

23:57.560 --> 24:01.180
So if you just cooked it like that, it would feel like a sponge full of air.

24:01.180 --> 24:05.340
So you have to eliminate the voids. This is the consolidation step.

24:06.000 --> 24:10.760
You take this coiled cylinder of strands, and you apply heat and physical compression.

24:11.240 --> 24:11.860
You press it.

24:12.500 --> 24:16.580
Under that heat and pressure, those round cylindrical strands are squeezed and deformed.

24:17.000 --> 24:22.180
They flatten against each other until they form irregular polygons that completely fill all the void space.

24:22.720 --> 24:26.060
And this leads to hypothesis 4, shell fusion.

24:26.480 --> 24:27.780
This is where the magic happens.

24:27.780 --> 24:34.120
The author hypothesizes that if the shell composition, the heat and the pressure are calibrated perfectly,

24:34.800 --> 24:38.660
the outer walls of all those individual strands will chemically bond.

24:39.080 --> 24:44.240
They will fuse together into a single, continuous, honeycomb-like protein network.

24:44.360 --> 24:47.080
But crucially, the inner cores remain distinct.

24:47.320 --> 24:51.900
Right. You have created a solid block of protein riddled with thousands of isolated,

24:52.120 --> 24:54.980
parallel, microscopic channels of fat and juice.

24:54.980 --> 24:57.120
It is a meticulously built matrix.

24:57.920 --> 25:01.380
The text measures the success of this with a metric called the fusion index.

25:01.720 --> 25:02.880
Right. The fusion index.

25:03.320 --> 25:05.200
You take two strands that have been pressed together,

25:05.440 --> 25:08.400
and you measure the peak mechanical force required to pull them apart.

25:08.600 --> 25:12.920
And you divide that by the force required to actually snap a single strand in half.

25:13.340 --> 25:18.300
You want the bond between the strands to be strong, but not stronger than the strand itself.

25:18.660 --> 25:23.020
Or you lose the fibrous texture entirely and just revert back to a homogenous paste.

25:23.020 --> 25:27.440
And here is where the mechanical engineering crosses over into culinary genius.

25:27.940 --> 25:31.360
The text introduces a concept called interlayer seasoning.

25:31.700 --> 25:32.880
I loved this part.

25:33.300 --> 25:37.500
What if you don't want the fusion index to be uniform throughout the entire block?

25:37.780 --> 25:38.320
Exactly.

25:38.660 --> 25:42.260
What if, while the traverse guide is winding the strands onto the spinning mandrel,

25:42.260 --> 25:50.080
You intentionally mist a fine layer of oil or dry spices or a non-binding starch paste between every fifth layer.

25:50.480 --> 25:52.740
By introducing a contaminant between the layers,

25:53.320 --> 25:57.860
you are intentionally disrupting the shell fusion in those specific, targeted locations.

25:58.520 --> 26:01.480
You are intentionally lowering the fusion index along that plane.

26:01.980 --> 26:03.960
You are literally programming the paramecium.

26:04.340 --> 26:05.760
You are telling the block of food,

26:06.200 --> 26:08.860
bond strongly here, but remain weak here.

26:08.860 --> 26:14.120
You are designing the exact macroscopic failure planes so that when a consumer attacks it with a fork,

26:14.280 --> 26:17.780
it falls apart in perfectly measured muscle-like bundles.

26:18.360 --> 26:20.360
It's not just mimicking the chemistry of meat.

26:20.480 --> 26:24.000
It's mimicking the exact architectural decay of meat under pressure.

26:24.400 --> 26:25.860
The precision is remarkable.

26:26.360 --> 26:29.040
But this immediately begs a massive logistical question.

26:29.320 --> 26:30.440
How to build it at scale.

26:30.820 --> 26:31.060
Right.

26:31.060 --> 26:35.620
How do you manufacture a highly complex, multi-layered, core shelf-used architecture

26:35.620 --> 26:40.860
without building a billion-dollar dedicated factory just to make one specific type of fake chicken?

26:41.300 --> 26:43.620
Because traditional food manufacturing is rigid.

26:44.260 --> 26:46.320
If you have a factory that makes extruded pasta,

26:46.620 --> 26:51.080
it has flour silos, water mixers, a massive extruder, and drying racks.

26:51.520 --> 26:53.800
And if the price of durum wheat triples due to a drought,

26:54.200 --> 26:56.760
you can't just run potatoes through that exact same line.

26:57.180 --> 26:58.080
The line shuts down.

26:58.260 --> 26:59.400
It's a total bottleneck.

26:59.400 --> 27:03.920
So to solve this, the text moves away from the microscopic physics of the strand

27:03.920 --> 27:07.440
and zooms out to the macro level of factory design.

27:07.780 --> 27:13.640
It proposes a manufacturing paradigm based on ingredient streams and an operator graph.

27:14.200 --> 27:18.460
If we connect this to the bigger picture, a factory isn't defined by the product it makes.

27:18.600 --> 27:20.820
It's defined by the operators it has.

27:21.340 --> 27:23.660
Operators are just unit operations,

27:24.140 --> 27:27.300
fundamental physical or chemical actions you can take on an ingredient.

27:27.300 --> 27:31.820
Wet milling, steeping, protein precipitation, centrifugation,

27:31.820 --> 27:35.040
extrusion, rolling compression, enzymatic fermentation.

27:35.380 --> 27:36.820
These are all operators.

27:37.400 --> 27:40.820
So how do you handle inputs if you aren't following a rigid recipe?

27:41.580 --> 27:46.840
The text describes a universal intake protocol based primarily on a centrifugal profile.

27:47.080 --> 27:50.940
This is a brilliant way to standardize chaotic biological inputs.

27:50.940 --> 27:58.020
Whether a truck delivers a load of yellow peas, oats, soybeans, or wild-forged cattail rhizomes,

27:58.540 --> 28:04.100
the first step is to mill a sample into a slurry with water and spin it in a high-speed centrifuge.

28:04.460 --> 28:07.620
And the centrifuge forces the slurry to separate based on density.

28:07.620 --> 28:10.040
You get distinct layers in the test tube.

28:10.040 --> 28:12.820
The heavy starch and fiber sediment drops to the bottom,

28:13.180 --> 28:14.160
the water sits in the middle,

28:14.440 --> 28:16.520
and the lighter oils float to the top.

28:17.080 --> 28:18.900
By measuring the volume of those layers,

28:19.120 --> 28:23.000
you instantly fingerprint the physical composition of that lot of ingredients.

28:23.440 --> 28:27.520
You know exactly what ratio of oil to water to starch you are dealing with.

28:28.140 --> 28:30.760
Once an ingredient is profiled, it becomes a stream.

28:31.440 --> 28:32.860
And instead of a recipe saying,

28:33.380 --> 28:35.300
use 100 pounds of soy to make this burger,

28:35.300 --> 28:38.340
a product route on the operator graph says,

28:38.960 --> 28:42.560
I need an ingredient stream that matches this specific centrifugal profile

28:42.560 --> 28:46.540
with this specific pH and this specific coagulation temperature.

28:47.320 --> 28:48.820
Pass it through the wet mill operator,

28:49.300 --> 28:50.540
then the centrifuge operator,

28:50.840 --> 28:52.080
then the coagulating operator.

28:52.580 --> 28:54.780
The resilience of this is staggering.

28:55.200 --> 28:58.840
If soy becomes too expensive or a blight wipes out a regional crop,

28:58.960 --> 29:00.260
the factory doesn't shut down.

29:00.620 --> 29:00.820
Nope.

29:00.920 --> 29:04.040
The software just looks for a local legume or grain combination

29:04.040 --> 29:07.120
that can be blended to match the required centrifugal profile

29:07.120 --> 29:08.440
for that operator route.

29:09.100 --> 29:12.320
It completely abstracts the agriculture away from the final product.

29:13.100 --> 29:17.040
This modularity allows for immense product diversity from a single facility.

29:17.560 --> 29:20.640
The operator graph demonstrates how the exact same equipment

29:20.640 --> 29:22.500
can yield vastly different foods.

29:23.040 --> 29:25.540
For instance, if your factory has a soaking tank,

29:25.780 --> 29:27.300
a wet mill, and an emulsifier,

29:27.920 --> 29:29.540
you route oats through those operators

29:29.540 --> 29:31.120
and output a commercial oat milk.

29:31.120 --> 29:33.800
But if you simply add a heating press

29:33.800 --> 29:35.860
and a coiling mandrel to your factory floor,

29:36.380 --> 29:39.720
you can take that exact same stream of oat and legume protein,

29:40.320 --> 29:42.200
route it away from the milk bottling line,

29:42.680 --> 29:44.660
push it through the core shell draw process,

29:45.140 --> 29:46.460
coil it, consolidate it,

29:46.820 --> 29:50.120
and output a sliceable, savory, shredding block

29:50.120 --> 29:51.620
of high-protein meat analog.

29:51.620 --> 29:53.580
The base is the same.

29:53.900 --> 29:55.940
The mechanical routing is what changes the food.

29:56.320 --> 29:57.200
But hold on.

29:57.780 --> 30:01.260
Let's look at the economics and the environmental reality of this.

30:01.820 --> 30:04.920
We are talking about heavy industrial fractionation.

30:05.140 --> 30:05.760
Oh, absolutely.

30:06.260 --> 30:08.280
You are taking thousands of pounds of peas,

30:08.840 --> 30:09.300
milling them,

30:09.760 --> 30:10.360
washing them,

30:10.820 --> 30:12.100
spinning them at high speeds

30:12.100 --> 30:15.380
just to extract the 20% that is pure protein isolate

30:15.380 --> 30:16.240
for your shell dough.

30:16.240 --> 30:18.520
What happens to the other 80%?

30:18.940 --> 30:20.720
The starch, the fiber, the hulls?

30:21.060 --> 30:22.840
If you were just throwing that in a landfill,

30:23.260 --> 30:24.980
this is an ecological disaster.

30:25.540 --> 30:26.740
Masquerading is innovation.

30:27.540 --> 30:29.880
The author is highly aware of the mass balance problem.

30:30.340 --> 30:32.840
The text mandates what it calls stream closure.

30:33.280 --> 30:34.120
Stream closure.

30:34.600 --> 30:38.060
So a regional manufacturing platform is only considered closed

30:38.060 --> 30:40.860
and, frankly, only economically viable

30:40.860 --> 30:43.280
when every single byproduct stream

30:43.280 --> 30:46.400
has a mathematically modeled, profitable destination.

30:47.120 --> 30:49.340
It's the old meatpacking adage applied to plants.

30:49.660 --> 30:51.280
Use everything but the squeal.

30:51.560 --> 30:52.080
Exactly.

30:52.520 --> 30:55.680
For example, when you extract protein from soybeans or peas

30:55.680 --> 30:56.700
to make milk or dough,

30:57.100 --> 30:59.200
you are left with a massive amount of wet,

30:59.400 --> 31:00.680
popy fiber called okara.

31:01.080 --> 31:02.300
In a closed platform,

31:02.440 --> 31:04.520
that okara stream is automatically routed

31:04.520 --> 31:05.440
to a different operator.

31:06.280 --> 31:07.920
It might be dried and milled

31:07.920 --> 31:09.980
into a high-fiber filler for baked goods

31:09.980 --> 31:12.300
or it might be routed into the core paste

31:12.300 --> 31:14.360
of the strands themselves as a bulking agent

31:14.360 --> 31:16.160
or, at the very least,

31:16.580 --> 31:18.300
stabilized for high-grade animal feed.

31:18.940 --> 31:20.060
And the dense starch sediment

31:20.060 --> 31:21.420
that drops out of the centrifuge?

31:21.580 --> 31:22.960
That gets pumped directly

31:22.960 --> 31:24.440
to a rolling hotplate operator

31:24.440 --> 31:25.920
to become the continuous dough

31:25.920 --> 31:27.380
for flatbreads or dumpling wrappers.

31:27.880 --> 31:28.780
Even the trimmings.

31:29.180 --> 31:32.040
When you press these massive cylindrical blocks

31:32.040 --> 31:33.020
of coiled strands,

31:33.560 --> 31:34.780
you have to square them off

31:34.780 --> 31:36.060
to put them in retail packaging.

31:36.740 --> 31:38.680
Those irregular trimmings are minced back

31:38.680 --> 31:40.220
into a paste and pumped right back

31:40.220 --> 31:40.960
into the draw tower

31:40.960 --> 31:41.980
to become the core filling

31:41.980 --> 31:43.060
for the next batch of strands.

31:43.060 --> 31:46.380
It creates completely internal closed loops.

31:46.380 --> 31:48.220
The financial math of building

31:48.220 --> 31:50.720
a decentralized regional processing facility

31:50.720 --> 31:52.380
only works if you monetize

31:52.380 --> 31:53.740
every single fraction

31:53.740 --> 31:54.820
of the agricultural input.

31:55.360 --> 31:56.580
You cannot afford waste.

31:57.100 --> 31:58.980
But this intense level of processing

31:58.980 --> 32:01.280
brings us to a massive societal debate.

32:01.680 --> 32:03.000
Milling, spinning, extreating,

32:03.240 --> 32:04.720
cross-linking with alginate, pressing.

32:05.100 --> 32:05.320
Right.

32:05.800 --> 32:07.440
We are essentially talking about

32:07.440 --> 32:08.580
the absolute peak

32:08.580 --> 32:10.600
of ultra-processed food.

32:10.600 --> 32:11.880
Are we just building

32:11.880 --> 32:13.360
a highly efficient platform

32:13.360 --> 32:14.980
for creating a new category

32:14.980 --> 32:17.080
of hyper-palatable junk food?

32:17.680 --> 32:18.960
So what does this all mean?

32:19.400 --> 32:22.180
The text confronts the UPF debate head-on,

32:22.480 --> 32:23.880
and it does so by citing

32:23.880 --> 32:25.580
empirical metabolic research.

32:26.420 --> 32:28.140
The author explicitly references

32:28.140 --> 32:30.440
the landmark randomized crossover trial

32:30.440 --> 32:32.760
conducted by Kevin Hall at the NIH,

32:33.320 --> 32:34.840
which is largely considered

32:34.840 --> 32:36.420
the gold standard study

32:36.420 --> 32:38.580
in the ultra-processed food conversation.

32:38.580 --> 32:40.500
This is the study where they literally

32:40.500 --> 32:41.940
locked 20 volunteers

32:41.940 --> 32:43.280
in a metabolic ward clinic

32:43.280 --> 32:44.120
for a month

32:44.120 --> 32:44.980
so they could control

32:44.980 --> 32:46.960
every single calorie they consumed.

32:47.340 --> 32:47.640
Yes.

32:48.080 --> 32:48.840
For two weeks,

32:48.940 --> 32:50.080
the subjects were fed a diet

32:50.080 --> 32:51.180
comprised entirely

32:51.180 --> 32:52.740
of ultra-processed foods.

32:53.240 --> 32:54.200
For the other two weeks,

32:54.520 --> 32:55.260
they were fed a diet

32:55.260 --> 32:56.960
of completely unprocessed,

32:57.040 --> 32:57.540
whole foods.

32:58.060 --> 33:00.060
And the researchers painstakingly

33:00.060 --> 33:01.000
matched the two diets

33:01.000 --> 33:01.540
on a spreadsheet.

33:01.720 --> 33:02.820
They had the exact same amount

33:02.820 --> 33:03.860
of calories, carbohydrates,

33:04.340 --> 33:05.980
fat, sugar, sodium, fiber,

33:06.200 --> 33:07.680
and protein presented to them.

33:07.680 --> 33:09.080
But the crucial part

33:09.080 --> 33:10.220
is that the subjects

33:10.220 --> 33:12.180
were instructed to eat ad lividum,

33:12.500 --> 33:13.920
eat as much or as little

33:13.920 --> 33:14.640
as they wanted

33:14.640 --> 33:15.660
until they were full.

33:16.640 --> 33:18.300
And the results were staggering.

33:19.140 --> 33:20.740
When eating the ultra-processed diet,

33:21.000 --> 33:22.120
the subjects consumed,

33:22.280 --> 33:23.000
on average,

33:23.440 --> 33:25.320
500 excess calories a day.

33:25.900 --> 33:27.180
They gained weight rapidly.

33:27.760 --> 33:29.460
When eating the whole food diet,

33:30.020 --> 33:30.740
they lost weight.

33:31.120 --> 33:32.620
Which proves that matching

33:32.620 --> 33:33.600
the macronutrients

33:33.600 --> 33:34.300
on a spreadsheet

33:34.300 --> 33:36.120
is entirely insufficient

33:36.120 --> 33:36.840
for predicting

33:36.840 --> 33:38.260
human metabolic behavior.

33:39.060 --> 33:40.060
A gram of carbohydrate

33:40.060 --> 33:41.160
in a whole apple

33:41.160 --> 33:42.240
does not trigger

33:42.240 --> 33:43.940
the same physiological response

33:43.940 --> 33:45.360
as a gram of carbohydrate

33:45.360 --> 33:46.360
in an extruded

33:46.360 --> 33:47.580
apple-flavored puff,

33:48.100 --> 33:49.260
even if the fiber content

33:49.260 --> 33:50.380
is perfectly matched.

33:51.120 --> 33:52.540
And the reason for this divergence

33:52.540 --> 33:54.300
is the physical architecture

33:54.300 --> 33:54.960
of the food.

33:55.480 --> 33:55.820
Right.

33:56.300 --> 33:57.960
The way a food is processed

33:57.960 --> 33:59.420
affects how much effort

33:59.420 --> 34:00.260
it takes to chew,

34:00.660 --> 34:02.040
how rapidly it empties

34:02.040 --> 34:02.600
from the stomach,

34:02.960 --> 34:03.840
and how quickly

34:03.840 --> 34:05.020
the nutrients are absorbed

34:05.020 --> 34:05.760
in the intestine.

34:06.480 --> 34:08.160
Which dictates insulin spikes

34:08.160 --> 34:09.580
and satiety hormones.

34:10.420 --> 34:11.300
Think about eating

34:11.300 --> 34:12.680
a handful of whole almonds.

34:13.400 --> 34:14.280
The fats and proteins

34:14.280 --> 34:15.360
are locked inside

34:15.360 --> 34:16.060
intact,

34:16.360 --> 34:17.920
microscopic plant cell walls.

34:18.580 --> 34:19.780
Your teeth can only crush

34:19.780 --> 34:21.020
so many of those walls.

34:21.440 --> 34:22.320
When you swallow,

34:22.560 --> 34:23.680
your digestive enzymes

34:23.680 --> 34:24.900
have to do the hard,

34:25.320 --> 34:26.460
time-consuming work

34:26.460 --> 34:27.280
of breaking down

34:27.280 --> 34:28.460
those cellular structures

34:28.460 --> 34:29.900
to access the calories.

34:29.900 --> 34:31.960
This requires energy,

34:32.280 --> 34:33.520
the thermic effect of food,

34:33.820 --> 34:35.120
and it slows down digestion,

34:35.300 --> 34:35.880
keeping you full.

34:36.920 --> 34:38.420
But most ultra-processed foods

34:38.420 --> 34:39.360
completely destroy

34:39.360 --> 34:40.340
that natural matrix.

34:40.840 --> 34:41.860
They pulverize the ingredients

34:41.860 --> 34:42.940
into a homogenous,

34:43.300 --> 34:44.280
pre-digested paste.

34:44.700 --> 34:45.400
You swallow it,

34:45.520 --> 34:46.680
instantly it hits your stomach,

34:46.900 --> 34:47.720
and the nutrients

34:47.720 --> 34:48.840
flood your bloodstream

34:48.840 --> 34:50.540
with zero mechanical resistance.

34:51.400 --> 34:52.460
So how on earth

34:52.460 --> 34:53.100
does the author

34:53.100 --> 34:54.220
defend the Strand Method,

34:54.680 --> 34:55.760
which involves milling

34:55.760 --> 34:56.860
and centrifuging everything

34:56.860 --> 34:58.060
into a paste to begin with?

34:58.060 --> 35:00.380
By drawing a very sharp

35:00.380 --> 35:01.360
technical distinction

35:01.360 --> 35:02.840
between a pulverized paste

35:02.840 --> 35:03.640
and a built matrix.

35:04.140 --> 35:05.300
Yes, the raw inputs

35:05.300 --> 35:06.160
are fractionated,

35:06.340 --> 35:07.440
but the entire purpose

35:07.440 --> 35:08.140
of the coiling

35:08.140 --> 35:09.360
and consolidation process

35:09.360 --> 35:10.320
is to build

35:10.320 --> 35:11.440
a new macro architecture.

35:12.440 --> 35:14.640
Ah, you're reconstructing

35:14.640 --> 35:15.300
the cell walls.

35:15.580 --> 35:16.180
Precisely.

35:16.440 --> 35:17.940
You have defined compartments,

35:18.320 --> 35:20.120
you have strong cross-linked shells

35:20.120 --> 35:21.200
protecting lipid cores,

35:21.480 --> 35:22.840
you have mechanical resistance

35:22.840 --> 35:24.020
via the helix angle.

35:24.020 --> 35:25.700
The text hypothesizes

35:25.700 --> 35:26.540
that by engineering

35:26.540 --> 35:28.460
this internal architecture,

35:28.940 --> 35:30.280
you can intentionally tune

35:30.280 --> 35:31.400
the mechanical breakdown

35:31.400 --> 35:32.060
of the food

35:32.060 --> 35:33.300
in the digestive tract.

35:33.820 --> 35:35.000
You can force the stomach

35:35.000 --> 35:36.300
and enzymes to work hard

35:36.300 --> 35:37.860
to breach the fused shells,

35:38.480 --> 35:39.420
effectively replicating

35:39.420 --> 35:40.800
the slow-digesting,

35:41.320 --> 35:42.480
high-satiety properties

35:42.480 --> 35:43.320
of whole foods.

35:43.600 --> 35:44.720
You are intentionally

35:44.720 --> 35:46.800
programming a high-thermic effect

35:46.800 --> 35:48.120
back into a processed food.

35:48.340 --> 35:50.480
That is a wild hypothesis,

35:50.780 --> 35:51.920
but the author insists

35:51.920 --> 35:53.560
it must be empirically measured,

35:53.560 --> 35:54.920
which is why the text mandates

35:54.920 --> 35:56.540
such intense testing protocols.

35:57.200 --> 35:58.360
You can't just taste

35:58.360 --> 35:59.280
the block of food and say,

35:59.480 --> 36:00.620
eh, it feels meaty.

36:01.020 --> 36:01.920
You have to prove

36:01.920 --> 36:03.800
the architecture mechanically works.

36:04.360 --> 36:05.580
The physical testing protocols

36:05.580 --> 36:06.660
are rigorous.

36:07.060 --> 36:08.040
We mentioned the texture

36:08.040 --> 36:09.000
profile analysis.

36:09.320 --> 36:10.620
This isn't a taste test.

36:11.040 --> 36:12.920
A machine uses a mechanical plunger

36:12.920 --> 36:14.620
to compress a uniform cube

36:14.620 --> 36:15.460
of the food twice.

36:15.720 --> 36:16.680
To perfectly mimic

36:16.680 --> 36:18.000
two bites of a human jaw.

36:18.380 --> 36:18.600
Right.

36:18.820 --> 36:20.300
And it measures the force curve

36:20.300 --> 36:21.020
of that compression.

36:21.440 --> 36:22.060
From that curve,

36:22.060 --> 36:23.260
you can mathematically derive

36:23.260 --> 36:24.460
values for hardness,

36:24.800 --> 36:25.340
cohesiveness,

36:25.680 --> 36:26.160
springiness,

36:26.320 --> 36:26.940
and chewiness.

36:27.420 --> 36:28.920
But the metric that really proves

36:28.920 --> 36:30.280
if the strand process worked

36:30.280 --> 36:32.320
is the anisotropy index.

36:32.780 --> 36:33.820
This is brilliant

36:33.820 --> 36:34.720
in its simplicity.

36:34.980 --> 36:35.720
Let's hear it.

36:36.720 --> 36:37.560
Let's think about

36:37.560 --> 36:38.740
the mechanics of our teeth.

36:39.220 --> 36:40.180
When you bite down

36:40.180 --> 36:41.320
on a standard hot dog,

36:41.700 --> 36:42.260
it doesn't matter

36:42.260 --> 36:43.680
what angle your teeth hit it.

36:44.060 --> 36:44.600
The resistance

36:44.600 --> 36:45.800
is exactly the same

36:45.800 --> 36:46.820
because a hot dog

36:46.820 --> 36:48.260
is an isotropic paste.

36:48.260 --> 36:49.920
It is completely uniform

36:49.920 --> 36:50.800
in all directions.

36:51.180 --> 36:52.120
But skeletal muscle

36:52.120 --> 36:53.420
is anisotropic.

36:53.700 --> 36:54.260
Exactly.

36:55.100 --> 36:55.780
So to measure

36:55.780 --> 36:56.900
the anisotropy index

36:56.900 --> 36:57.760
of the manufactured

36:57.760 --> 36:58.520
strand block,

36:59.020 --> 37:00.520
you use an instrumented blade

37:00.520 --> 37:01.640
to shear the block

37:01.640 --> 37:03.040
parallel to the direction

37:03.040 --> 37:04.180
the strands were laid down.

37:04.720 --> 37:05.700
You record the force.

37:06.060 --> 37:07.160
Then you turn the block

37:07.160 --> 37:07.940
90 degrees

37:07.940 --> 37:08.920
and you shear it

37:08.920 --> 37:09.980
across the strands,

37:10.440 --> 37:11.200
essentially cutting

37:11.200 --> 37:12.020
the fibers in half.

37:12.140 --> 37:13.460
You divide the parallel force

37:13.460 --> 37:14.660
by the across force.

37:15.120 --> 37:16.620
If the material is a paste,

37:16.620 --> 37:17.820
like the hot dog,

37:18.140 --> 37:19.840
the ratio is exactly 1.

37:20.340 --> 37:21.060
But if the ratio

37:21.060 --> 37:22.420
drops well below 1,

37:22.660 --> 37:23.520
it means the food

37:23.520 --> 37:24.560
yields much easier

37:24.560 --> 37:26.060
along a specific axis.

37:26.520 --> 37:27.680
It proves that you

37:27.680 --> 37:28.400
have successfully

37:28.400 --> 37:29.560
engineered a grain.

37:29.940 --> 37:30.640
You've created

37:30.640 --> 37:31.900
a directional structure.

37:32.380 --> 37:33.240
And they don't stop

37:33.240 --> 37:34.260
at mechanical testing.

37:34.780 --> 37:35.720
To prove the late

37:35.720 --> 37:36.780
binding hypothesis,

37:37.140 --> 37:37.760
the idea that

37:37.760 --> 37:38.580
the matrix graphics

37:38.580 --> 37:39.740
can hide the carrier

37:39.740 --> 37:40.560
physics engine,

37:41.120 --> 37:41.700
they mandate

37:41.700 --> 37:43.300
sensory triangle tests.

37:43.720 --> 37:44.840
In a triangle test,

37:44.840 --> 37:46.120
you present a blinded

37:46.120 --> 37:46.720
human panel

37:46.720 --> 37:48.020
with three coated samples.

37:48.520 --> 37:49.320
Two are identical

37:49.320 --> 37:50.920
and one is the variable.

37:51.340 --> 37:51.740
For example,

37:51.940 --> 37:52.860
two samples might have

37:52.860 --> 37:53.700
a carrier core

37:53.700 --> 37:54.760
made of lentil protein

37:54.760 --> 37:56.180
and one has a core

37:56.180 --> 37:57.300
made of oat protein.

37:57.640 --> 37:58.840
But all three are coated

37:58.840 --> 37:59.960
in the exact same

37:59.960 --> 38:01.720
complex matrix sauce

38:01.720 --> 38:03.500
and cooked identical ways.

38:03.940 --> 38:04.740
The panelist simply

38:04.740 --> 38:05.360
has to pick out

38:05.360 --> 38:06.120
which one is different.

38:06.500 --> 38:07.540
The scientific rigor

38:07.540 --> 38:08.940
applied here is crucial.

38:09.320 --> 38:10.080
The text warns

38:10.080 --> 38:10.860
that a null result

38:10.860 --> 38:11.760
in a triangle test,

38:12.220 --> 38:12.840
meaning the panel

38:12.840 --> 38:13.980
was completely unable

38:13.980 --> 38:15.260
to pick the odd one out

38:15.260 --> 38:15.960
at a rate better

38:15.960 --> 38:17.060
than statistical guessing,

38:17.580 --> 38:18.680
does not prove

38:18.680 --> 38:20.200
that the two underlying

38:20.200 --> 38:21.080
foods are identical.

38:21.680 --> 38:23.720
It is a contextual equivalence.

38:24.100 --> 38:24.760
It just proves

38:24.760 --> 38:25.340
that under those

38:25.340 --> 38:26.740
specific matrix conditions,

38:27.180 --> 38:27.980
with that specific

38:27.980 --> 38:29.140
configural aroma,

38:29.840 --> 38:31.220
the human sensory apparatus

38:31.220 --> 38:32.700
cannot differentiate them.

38:32.960 --> 38:33.840
And this loops back

38:33.840 --> 38:35.300
to the centrifugal profile.

38:35.840 --> 38:36.900
Just because two streams

38:36.900 --> 38:37.680
of different legumes

38:37.680 --> 38:39.000
have the exact same ratio

38:39.000 --> 38:40.680
of fat to water to solids

38:40.680 --> 38:42.120
when you spin them in a tube

38:42.120 --> 38:43.300
doesn't mean they will

38:43.300 --> 38:44.200
behave identically

38:44.200 --> 38:45.240
when drawn into a fiber.

38:46.140 --> 38:47.560
Equivalence is a moving target

38:47.560 --> 38:48.660
depending on what level

38:48.660 --> 38:49.380
of the architecture

38:49.380 --> 38:50.140
you are measuring,

38:50.400 --> 38:52.580
which perfectly transitions us

38:52.580 --> 38:53.180
into the final

38:53.180 --> 38:55.320
and perhaps most unexpected

38:55.320 --> 38:57.000
argument in the entire monograph.

38:57.500 --> 38:58.860
Oh, this is a big pivot.

38:59.360 --> 39:01.300
After spending hundreds of pages

39:01.300 --> 39:02.420
on fluid dynamics,

39:02.660 --> 39:03.480
draw ratios,

39:03.840 --> 39:04.500
void spaces,

39:04.680 --> 39:05.780
and sheer force mathematics,

39:06.380 --> 39:07.860
the text abruptly shifts

39:07.860 --> 39:09.040
into a normative argument

39:09.040 --> 39:10.900
about global socioeconomics.

39:11.540 --> 39:12.660
The concluding chapter

39:12.660 --> 39:13.260
is titled

39:13.260 --> 39:15.880
Availability Before Restriction,

39:16.000 --> 39:17.080
and it acts as

39:17.080 --> 39:18.180
the ethical justification

39:18.180 --> 39:19.620
for why this massive

39:19.620 --> 39:20.360
engineering effort

39:20.360 --> 39:21.820
is necessary in the first place.

39:22.200 --> 39:23.500
It makes an economic

39:23.500 --> 39:24.660
and moral argument

39:24.660 --> 39:26.060
about how we handle

39:26.060 --> 39:27.300
the environmental fallout

39:27.300 --> 39:28.440
of the global food system.

39:29.340 --> 39:30.580
We know that industrial,

39:30.800 --> 39:31.540
factory, farmed,

39:31.560 --> 39:32.340
animal agriculture

39:32.340 --> 39:33.780
is a massive driver

39:33.780 --> 39:35.100
of greenhouse gas emissions,

39:35.560 --> 39:36.340
land degradation,

39:36.700 --> 39:37.620
and water use.

39:37.860 --> 39:39.600
The standard policy response

39:39.600 --> 39:40.180
being debated

39:40.180 --> 39:41.000
usually involves

39:41.000 --> 39:42.100
taxing meat,

39:42.300 --> 39:43.160
reducing subsidies,

39:43.460 --> 39:44.820
or legally restricting supply

39:44.820 --> 39:45.800
to force a shift

39:45.800 --> 39:46.780
in consumer behavior.

39:47.580 --> 39:48.000
This raises

39:48.000 --> 39:49.260
an important question, though.

39:50.000 --> 39:50.840
The author argues

39:50.840 --> 39:51.540
that implementing

39:51.540 --> 39:52.420
those restrictions

39:52.420 --> 39:53.500
before a viable

39:53.500 --> 39:55.220
one-to-one structural replacement

39:55.220 --> 39:56.340
is widely available

39:56.340 --> 39:58.100
is fundamentally unjust.

39:58.840 --> 39:59.780
It's a profound point.

39:59.980 --> 40:00.940
It's a profound point.

40:01.260 --> 40:02.100
You can't just slap

40:02.100 --> 40:03.380
a massive carbon tax

40:03.380 --> 40:04.000
on ground beef

40:04.000 --> 40:05.880
and tell lower-income populations,

40:05.880 --> 40:07.260
sorry, you just have to

40:07.260 --> 40:08.260
stop eating cheap burgers

40:08.260 --> 40:09.240
and start eating

40:09.240 --> 40:10.120
boiled lentils.

40:10.520 --> 40:11.520
Not without providing

40:11.520 --> 40:12.120
an alternative

40:12.120 --> 40:12.660
that gives them

40:12.660 --> 40:13.680
the exact same

40:13.680 --> 40:14.800
sensory satisfaction,

40:15.500 --> 40:16.620
the same mechanical chew,

40:16.740 --> 40:18.780
and the same culinary utility

40:18.780 --> 40:20.200
in their cultural dishes.

40:20.200 --> 40:21.780
If you merely restrict

40:21.780 --> 40:23.140
the supply of a culturally

40:23.140 --> 40:24.100
foundational food

40:24.100 --> 40:24.840
without providing

40:24.840 --> 40:26.640
a technically equivalent substitute,

40:27.080 --> 40:27.880
you aren't solving

40:27.880 --> 40:28.620
a climate crisis.

40:28.880 --> 40:30.080
You are simply causing

40:30.080 --> 40:30.780
a massive,

40:31.000 --> 40:32.160
regressive deflation

40:32.160 --> 40:33.040
of living standards

40:33.040 --> 40:33.980
for the demographics

40:33.980 --> 40:35.380
least able to afford

40:35.380 --> 40:35.980
the transition.

40:35.980 --> 40:37.100
And historically,

40:37.300 --> 40:38.240
that kind of policy

40:38.240 --> 40:39.400
provokes intense

40:39.400 --> 40:40.460
societal resistance

40:40.460 --> 40:41.580
that ends up

40:41.580 --> 40:42.580
defeating the environmental

40:42.580 --> 40:43.340
goals anyway.

40:44.380 --> 40:45.420
The author makes

40:45.420 --> 40:46.160
a compelling case

40:46.160 --> 40:47.800
that texture and flavor

40:47.800 --> 40:50.100
are not superficial luxuries.

40:50.300 --> 40:51.160
No, they really aren't.

40:51.660 --> 40:52.780
The way food bites back

40:52.780 --> 40:53.540
against your teeth,

40:53.840 --> 40:55.280
the specific burst of fat

40:55.280 --> 40:56.220
that coats your palate,

40:56.760 --> 40:57.380
the complex,

40:57.820 --> 40:59.400
configural aroma of a dish

40:59.400 --> 41:00.040
that your family

41:00.040 --> 41:01.060
has cooked for generations,

41:01.580 --> 41:02.820
these aren't just

41:02.820 --> 41:03.720
nice-to-haves.

41:03.720 --> 41:05.440
They are deep psychological

41:05.440 --> 41:06.840
and cultural anchors.

41:07.000 --> 41:08.880
They are human necessities.

41:09.400 --> 41:09.760
Therefore,

41:09.900 --> 41:10.940
the engineering challenges

41:10.940 --> 41:12.420
outlined in this document,

41:13.040 --> 41:14.040
solving the Rayleigh

41:14.040 --> 41:14.940
plateau instability

41:14.940 --> 41:15.980
of a fluid core,

41:16.500 --> 41:17.800
balancing the cross-linking

41:17.800 --> 41:18.920
of an alginate shell,

41:19.380 --> 41:20.980
mastering the helical geometry

41:20.980 --> 41:21.600
of coiling

41:21.600 --> 41:23.040
to program weak planes,

41:23.980 --> 41:25.080
these are not just

41:25.080 --> 41:25.720
clever tricks

41:25.720 --> 41:26.940
to make a more expensive

41:26.940 --> 41:27.660
veggie burger

41:27.660 --> 41:28.900
for upscale restaurants

41:28.900 --> 41:29.660
in major cities.

41:30.060 --> 41:31.360
They are the technical

41:31.360 --> 41:32.200
prerequisites

41:32.200 --> 41:33.340
for societal change.

41:33.340 --> 41:34.240
It frames solving

41:34.240 --> 41:34.700
the physics

41:34.700 --> 41:36.060
of a core shell strand

41:36.060 --> 41:36.900
as a literal

41:36.900 --> 41:37.920
moral imperative.

41:38.920 --> 41:40.080
Engineering the perfect,

41:40.280 --> 41:40.760
accessible,

41:41.060 --> 41:42.540
modular plant-based shred

41:42.540 --> 41:44.140
is the required key

41:44.140 --> 41:44.700
to unlocking

41:44.700 --> 41:46.240
global food system reform

41:46.240 --> 41:47.500
without punishing

41:47.500 --> 41:48.320
the global poor.

41:48.840 --> 41:50.120
It completely reframes

41:50.120 --> 41:50.640
how you read

41:50.640 --> 41:51.300
the entire

41:51.300 --> 41:52.260
preceding document.

41:52.480 --> 41:52.940
It isn't just

41:52.940 --> 41:53.760
a patent application

41:53.760 --> 41:54.800
for a factory machine.

41:55.180 --> 41:56.840
It is a mission statement

41:56.840 --> 41:57.860
for food justice

41:57.860 --> 41:58.720
disguised as

41:58.720 --> 42:00.440
a dense physics text.

42:00.440 --> 42:02.000
We have covered

42:02.000 --> 42:03.020
an absolutely

42:03.020 --> 42:04.100
immense amount

42:04.100 --> 42:04.820
of ground today.

42:05.280 --> 42:06.180
From the microscopic

42:06.180 --> 42:07.320
capillary breakup

42:07.320 --> 42:08.520
of an alginate-coated

42:08.520 --> 42:09.500
emulsion under tension

42:09.500 --> 42:11.200
to the macrogeometry

42:11.200 --> 42:12.180
of filament-wound

42:12.180 --> 42:13.080
cross-layers

42:13.080 --> 42:14.320
to the supply chain

42:14.320 --> 42:15.040
resilience of a

42:15.040 --> 42:16.040
closed-loop operator

42:16.040 --> 42:16.780
graph factory

42:16.780 --> 42:18.120
and finally to the

42:18.120 --> 42:19.440
socioeconomic philosophy

42:19.440 --> 42:20.240
of availability

42:20.240 --> 42:21.380
before restriction.

42:21.380 --> 42:22.720
It forces you

42:22.720 --> 42:23.380
to completely

42:23.380 --> 42:24.460
reassess the food

42:24.460 --> 42:25.120
in your pantry.

42:26.040 --> 42:26.540
It suggests

42:26.540 --> 42:27.240
that the future

42:27.240 --> 42:27.580
of eating

42:27.580 --> 42:28.080
might look

42:28.080 --> 42:29.020
significantly less

42:29.020 --> 42:29.760
like a traditional

42:29.760 --> 42:30.300
farm

42:30.300 --> 42:31.540
and much more

42:31.540 --> 42:32.200
like a highly

42:32.200 --> 42:32.940
configurable,

42:33.340 --> 42:33.960
hyper-efficient

42:33.960 --> 42:34.920
material science

42:34.920 --> 42:35.460
platform.

42:35.960 --> 42:36.640
But crucially,

42:36.840 --> 42:37.500
a platform that

42:37.500 --> 42:38.300
uses intense

42:38.300 --> 42:39.140
technology to

42:39.140 --> 42:39.880
respect and

42:39.880 --> 42:40.500
replicate the

42:40.500 --> 42:41.300
deep human

42:41.300 --> 42:42.220
biological need

42:42.220 --> 42:43.260
for a satisfying,

42:43.560 --> 42:44.100
mechanically

42:44.100 --> 42:44.960
complex meal.

42:45.220 --> 42:45.880
It's acknowledging

42:45.880 --> 42:46.540
that we cannot

42:46.540 --> 42:47.380
overcome our

42:47.380 --> 42:48.500
biological hardware,

42:48.900 --> 42:49.400
our teeth,

42:49.680 --> 42:50.260
our digestive

42:50.260 --> 42:50.800
tracts,

42:51.380 --> 42:52.280
our olfactory

42:52.280 --> 42:52.700
limits.

42:53.240 --> 42:53.760
So we must

42:53.760 --> 42:54.260
adapt the

42:54.260 --> 42:54.980
software of our

42:54.980 --> 42:55.480
food to meet

42:55.480 --> 42:56.120
those exact

42:56.120 --> 42:56.900
mechanical

42:56.900 --> 42:57.720
specifications,

42:58.100 --> 42:59.060
regardless of

42:59.060 --> 42:59.860
what seeds or

42:59.860 --> 43:00.760
legumes we use

43:00.760 --> 43:01.280
to build it.

43:01.920 --> 43:02.440
Which is pretty

43:02.440 --> 43:03.080
incredible when

43:03.080 --> 43:03.700
you think about it.

43:04.460 --> 43:05.520
Which leaves me

43:05.520 --> 43:06.400
with one final,

43:06.900 --> 43:07.820
deeply mind-bending

43:07.820 --> 43:08.580
thought to mull

43:08.580 --> 43:08.820
over.

43:09.540 --> 43:10.400
The entire

43:10.400 --> 43:11.300
premise of this

43:11.300 --> 43:12.460
monograph is using

43:12.460 --> 43:13.480
this platform to

43:13.480 --> 43:14.120
replicate the

43:14.120 --> 43:14.860
experience of

43:14.860 --> 43:15.600
animal muscle

43:15.600 --> 43:16.680
or whole fruit.

43:17.500 --> 43:18.100
But if this

43:18.100 --> 43:19.080
platform truly

43:19.080 --> 43:19.800
masters the

43:19.800 --> 43:20.320
independent,

43:20.820 --> 43:21.640
modular control

43:21.640 --> 43:22.760
of texture via

43:22.760 --> 43:23.980
strand geometry,

43:24.240 --> 43:25.160
fusion indexes,

43:25.240 --> 43:26.000
and helix angles,

43:26.600 --> 43:27.340
and completely

43:27.340 --> 43:28.980
masters flavor via

43:28.980 --> 43:29.580
late-binding

43:29.580 --> 43:30.320
chemistry and

43:30.320 --> 43:31.280
configural perception,

43:32.180 --> 43:32.880
why stop at

43:32.880 --> 43:33.560
mimicking what

43:33.560 --> 43:34.540
already exists?

43:35.360 --> 43:36.060
That is the

43:36.060 --> 43:36.700
frontier this

43:36.700 --> 43:37.860
technology inevitably

43:37.860 --> 43:38.560
opens up.

43:38.900 --> 43:39.200
Right.

43:39.880 --> 43:40.800
If the physics

43:40.800 --> 43:41.760
engine is completely

43:41.760 --> 43:42.660
divorced from the

43:42.660 --> 43:43.500
graphics rendering,

43:43.980 --> 43:44.780
why just render a

43:44.780 --> 43:45.080
cow?

43:45.960 --> 43:46.720
Once we

43:46.720 --> 43:47.900
completely detach the

43:47.900 --> 43:48.760
experience of eating

43:48.760 --> 43:49.920
from the evolutionary

43:49.920 --> 43:51.160
constraints of plants

43:51.160 --> 43:51.660
and animals,

43:52.220 --> 43:53.240
we aren't bound by

43:53.240 --> 43:54.180
biology anymore.

43:54.920 --> 43:55.860
Will we start inventing

43:55.860 --> 43:56.600
entirely new,

43:56.860 --> 43:58.040
unimaginable categories

43:58.040 --> 43:58.460
of food?

43:59.040 --> 44:00.100
Textures and flavors

44:00.100 --> 44:01.040
that have literally

44:01.040 --> 44:02.080
never existed in

44:02.080 --> 44:02.700
nature before.

44:03.020 --> 44:04.100
A structure that

44:04.100 --> 44:05.060
shreds with the

44:05.060 --> 44:06.100
precise mechanical

44:06.100 --> 44:07.400
resistance of slow-cooked

44:07.400 --> 44:09.100
pork, but melts at

44:09.100 --> 44:09.920
body temperature like

44:09.920 --> 44:11.680
ice cream, and tastes

44:11.680 --> 44:13.040
like a configural

44:13.040 --> 44:14.060
spice blend we don't

44:14.060 --> 44:14.940
even have a name for

44:14.940 --> 44:15.200
yet.

44:15.200 --> 44:16.620
When you abstract

44:16.620 --> 44:17.800
food down to pure

44:17.800 --> 44:18.580
operators and

44:18.580 --> 44:19.920
variables, the design

44:19.920 --> 44:21.100
space ceases to be

44:21.100 --> 44:21.620
agricultural.

44:22.060 --> 44:23.300
It becomes infinite.

44:24.040 --> 44:25.000
Our pleats might no

44:25.000 --> 44:26.140
longer have a botanical

44:26.140 --> 44:27.540
or zoological pedigree

44:27.540 --> 44:27.940
at all.

44:28.540 --> 44:29.980
The origin story won't

44:29.980 --> 44:30.620
be a farm.

44:31.000 --> 44:32.000
It will be an

44:32.000 --> 44:32.640
architecture.

44:33.400 --> 44:34.180
Thank you for joining

44:34.180 --> 44:35.300
us on this incredibly

44:35.300 --> 44:35.960
deep dive.

44:36.560 --> 44:37.540
Keep questioning the

44:37.540 --> 44:38.240
blueprints of our

44:38.240 --> 44:39.460
changing world, and

44:39.460 --> 44:40.100
we'll see you next

44:40.100 --> 44:40.340
time.

44:40.340 --> 45:10.320
Thank you.

